WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.320
I'm Steve Paulson, and we are back with theoretical physicist Carlo Rivelli, who has been thinking

00:09.320 --> 00:13.620
about why he was consumed by questions about ultimate reality when he was young.

00:15.760 --> 00:19.760
Yes, I had a difficult adolescence.

00:20.400 --> 00:22.880
I disliked the adults around me.

00:23.340 --> 00:25.740
I couldn't understand why they were doing what they were doing.

00:25.740 --> 00:33.020
Everybody had great, beautiful ideals to talk about, and they seemed to me all to be fake

00:33.020 --> 00:34.860
and incredibly hypocritical.

00:35.420 --> 00:38.840
So I was very confused about the world, about everything.

00:38.960 --> 00:40.760
And this confusion grew, in a sense.

00:41.060 --> 00:46.800
And the desire of putting order and understanding was very strong.

00:47.100 --> 00:53.120
And I remember that I had this strong sense, there should be a truth somewhere.

00:53.280 --> 00:54.440
I want to go for the truth.

00:54.440 --> 00:56.140
This has been a...

00:56.140 --> 01:01.860
If I read back, I have been writing all my life a lot, just for myself, notebook and notebook.

01:02.520 --> 01:08.880
And this idea, I want to try to go for the truth, it's all over my writing.

01:10.320 --> 01:12.040
I don't want to fake it.

01:12.360 --> 01:13.780
I don't want to become hypocritical.

01:13.920 --> 01:14.880
And I want to search.

01:15.500 --> 01:17.120
That was also the spirit of the time.

01:17.180 --> 01:17.960
We were all searching.

01:18.660 --> 01:19.400
This is all in search.

01:19.400 --> 01:23.460
But I'm sure you're searching now, but it sounds like it's a very different way now.

01:23.460 --> 01:24.660
So I'm trying to figure out...

01:24.660 --> 01:25.160
It is a big...

01:25.160 --> 01:26.520
What was the nature of that search?

01:26.520 --> 01:27.140
I'm getting there.

01:27.200 --> 01:27.940
I'm getting there.

01:28.440 --> 01:29.340
I'm still searching, right?

01:29.440 --> 01:30.440
I'm paid for search.

01:30.520 --> 01:33.740
I was so lucky that then I got a job to search.

01:35.080 --> 01:37.280
But I'm not searching for the ultimate reality.

01:37.640 --> 01:39.540
I'm searching for understanding something more.

01:40.080 --> 01:46.500
And I think the idea of the ultimate reality in science, like in philosophy, it's a bad idea.

01:46.500 --> 01:51.120
Nowadays, I'm more and more interested in philosophy.

01:51.380 --> 01:53.000
I go to philosophy conferences.

01:53.240 --> 01:55.460
This is very philosophical, this meeting.

01:56.140 --> 01:58.260
I'm invited by philosophy department.

01:58.440 --> 01:59.540
I write in philosophy journals.

01:59.740 --> 02:01.140
And I read a huge amount of philosophy.

02:01.820 --> 02:10.280
And the idea of getting to the bottom, understanding the ultimate nature of reality, what is nature, what is fundamental?

02:10.280 --> 02:17.620
It's matter, spirit, it's God, it's perception, it's consciousness, it's language.

02:18.420 --> 02:19.900
None of this is fundamental.

02:20.880 --> 02:24.100
It's like when we're talking about music before, right?

02:24.100 --> 02:24.740
I'm not a musician.

02:24.920 --> 02:26.900
Suppose I was a musician.

02:27.260 --> 02:29.240
I want to write better and better music.

02:29.620 --> 02:31.360
Do I want to write the ultimate music?

02:31.520 --> 02:33.020
No, there's no ultimate music.

02:33.240 --> 02:34.500
But there is better music.

02:34.500 --> 02:37.040
And I think there is better knowledge.

02:38.020 --> 02:40.140
Einstein is better than Newton.

02:40.540 --> 02:41.380
Newton is very good.

02:41.480 --> 02:43.580
It's super, super good and still used a lot.

02:43.740 --> 02:44.380
Einstein is better.

02:44.800 --> 02:46.680
It's a deeper look into reality.

02:47.180 --> 02:51.580
And if all goes right, quantum gravity is a little step ahead.

02:53.920 --> 02:57.100
Can't we be happy with lack of knowledge?

02:57.960 --> 03:01.760
Was that something that came, this appreciation of not knowing?

03:01.980 --> 03:02.180
Yeah.

03:02.680 --> 03:04.100
Did that come with age?

03:04.100 --> 03:07.660
With age, maybe it's just, you know, you get relaxed with age.

03:08.980 --> 03:14.520
I'm asking this out of genuine curiosity because I'll tell you a little bit about my own personal history.

03:14.660 --> 03:23.280
So when I was 17, 18, 19, I was obsessed with the existential writers, you know, those kind of depressing European writers.

03:23.540 --> 03:27.240
Camus, Dostoevsky, Kafka, you know, all those guys.

03:28.000 --> 03:31.820
Like The Myth of Sisyphus by Camus is sort of this foundational book for me.

03:31.820 --> 03:35.380
And that quest for meaning was like hugely important.

03:35.660 --> 03:36.740
I agonized over that.

03:37.520 --> 03:38.920
And I don't anymore.

03:39.120 --> 03:40.720
And I've puzzled over that.

03:40.860 --> 03:43.220
I mean, you know, maybe I'm, maybe I'm wiser now.

03:43.380 --> 03:44.760
That's a lot of lived experience.

03:44.920 --> 03:47.660
Since then, you know, I can sort of rationalize this.

03:47.660 --> 03:52.680
Or maybe I'm just a little jaded and life isn't as raw as it once was.

03:52.760 --> 03:54.580
I'm kind of a little more comfortable.

03:54.580 --> 03:59.420
And I don't maybe feel like I need to ask those big questions anymore.

03:59.520 --> 04:06.160
So I'm interested in your response partly because you're interested in time and age and, you know, time go together.

04:06.160 --> 04:10.160
And I'm just wondering, yeah, we're wiser, I guess, as we're older.

04:10.320 --> 04:16.220
But do we also lose something when we no longer have that rawness of being young?

04:16.920 --> 04:17.360
Yeah.

04:18.720 --> 04:19.160
Yeah.

04:19.460 --> 04:20.200
I don't know.

04:20.300 --> 04:22.380
I don't know which one of these is.

04:22.440 --> 04:24.460
It's probably a combination of those.

04:24.560 --> 04:26.880
And I actually have asked the same question to myself.

04:27.560 --> 04:28.620
To some extent, yes.

04:28.620 --> 04:33.440
You know, I'm much older and happily.

04:34.500 --> 04:34.940
Right.

04:35.000 --> 04:36.720
I would never want to go back to those.

04:36.920 --> 04:37.180
Yeah.

04:38.440 --> 04:44.100
It's nothing hard as being a 20-year-old boy, especially if girls are not.

04:45.320 --> 04:48.780
And life has been nice.

04:49.200 --> 04:53.720
And I'm more content with what I have.

04:53.920 --> 04:54.720
So that's one thing.

04:54.800 --> 04:56.000
Another thing is historical.

04:56.240 --> 04:58.160
I mean, that period I remember very well.

04:58.160 --> 05:02.480
I also was reading Sartre and Camus and all this anguish.

05:02.880 --> 05:06.780
And Tostoyevsky was my absolute, is still now my preferred writer.

05:07.420 --> 05:13.700
That was the after Second World War, when Europe had just committed a dramatic suicide in two acts,

05:13.880 --> 05:15.040
First and Second World War.

05:15.580 --> 05:20.600
The European intellectual scene was totally confused and desperate.

05:20.800 --> 05:21.500
What have we done?

05:21.500 --> 05:27.640
We thought we were the pinnacle of civilization and master of the world and ourselves.

05:27.640 --> 05:32.560
And the only thing we were able to do is to massacre one another and destroy the continent.

05:32.560 --> 05:39.080
I mean, this is a moment of profound spiritual dispersion and being lost.

05:39.860 --> 05:40.880
The times have changed.

05:41.280 --> 05:42.520
We're not in much better times.

05:42.600 --> 05:44.780
We're going probably to the same horror again.

05:44.780 --> 05:52.880
But besides the two things, I think it was a real intellectual step ahead for me.

05:53.740 --> 06:02.160
Reading philosophers like Wittgenstein or Nagarjuna, a great Buddhist philosopher, had a big, big intellectual impact on me.

06:02.160 --> 06:08.760
And both of them, from completely different traditions, from completely different, two millennia of difference between the two,

06:09.300 --> 06:16.740
in common, they have this message, which is, you know, be very careful because you're asking questions which are meaningless.

06:17.000 --> 06:19.920
And you're torturing yourself on questions which are meaningless.

06:19.920 --> 06:27.100
So one thing that some people who are sort of searching for that, I don't want to call it ultimate reality, but deeper reality,

06:27.500 --> 06:30.960
is they seek out transcendent experiences.

06:31.260 --> 06:32.460
I mean, it could be psychedelics.

06:33.000 --> 06:34.660
It could be something else.

06:35.040 --> 06:40.960
And does transcendence, does that have resonance for you or transcendent experiences, are those important to you?

06:40.960 --> 06:51.460
I was thinking this morning, because I was talking about that, there are certain experiences which are, you know, powerful, changing,

06:51.940 --> 06:54.280
taking you outside your normal reality.

06:55.380 --> 07:00.700
There are mystical experiences, there are psychedelic experiences, or even, you know, when you're completely in love.

07:01.120 --> 07:07.840
So we have an everyday life we go through and then something breaks and we see something completely new, completely different,

07:07.980 --> 07:09.680
elsewhere, it's true.

07:09.680 --> 07:20.640
Now, is this transcendence in the sense that I should think of the world as the material reality here, which I see every day,

07:20.760 --> 07:23.820
and something else, which I rarely, no, no, no.

07:23.880 --> 07:32.700
I don't think that there is any substantial metaphysical break of the world between immanent and transcendent,

07:32.840 --> 07:37.660
between mind and matter, between spirit and body.

07:37.660 --> 07:44.780
So going back to, like, your first LSD experience when you were 17, it changed the way you think.

07:44.960 --> 07:45.120
Yeah.

07:45.460 --> 07:50.660
But it didn't, you don't necessarily think it sort of put you in touch with some deeper sense of reality.

07:50.660 --> 08:01.200
Not anymore than the way the first time I looked into the microscope and what looked like water,

08:01.740 --> 08:04.960
to my astonishment, everybody's gone through that.

08:06.040 --> 08:10.380
You know, there's little bugs moving there, there's incredible richness of things happening.

08:10.380 --> 08:16.420
And wow, I've, yes, I've seen more of reality, but it's not transcendent.

08:16.500 --> 08:18.800
It's just, you know, bugs down there swimming in the water.

08:19.120 --> 08:21.340
It's amazement is what it sounds like.

08:21.340 --> 08:21.620
It's amazement.

08:21.800 --> 08:30.000
It's amazement and it's the realization that reality is far more complex than what we view in our day life.

08:30.000 --> 08:31.760
So I think this is a point.

08:32.400 --> 08:39.840
If we think that what reality is, is this cup, this table, you and I, a conversation, day and night,

08:39.980 --> 08:45.260
and that's basically what the world is about, we're completely wrong, okay?

08:45.720 --> 08:50.700
That's the world, what the world is completely wrong for us at our scale at this, you know,

08:50.760 --> 08:53.880
one meter, kilometers, hundred kilometers, a few years.

08:53.880 --> 09:01.300
But at just the scale of the microscope would see, or quantum gravity would see, or a cosmos,

09:02.400 --> 09:03.720
reality is far more complicated.

09:03.940 --> 09:04.860
There are all these layers.

09:05.220 --> 09:10.420
So once you realize that there are all these layers, all this complexity, all this,

09:11.180 --> 09:17.360
to split the world into an immanent and a transcendent, it's just silly.

09:17.680 --> 09:19.680
I mean, there's more richness than that.

09:20.380 --> 09:21.360
So I want to come back.

09:21.360 --> 09:24.980
I want to pick up a thread from earlier in the conversation, going back to politics.

09:25.260 --> 09:25.440
Okay.

09:25.540 --> 09:29.020
And you were saying that, you know, you're active student radical.

09:29.340 --> 09:33.840
At a certain point, you just felt like you and your movement had failed.

09:34.140 --> 09:38.520
And so you went into physics at that point, you know, maybe changing the world in that sense.

09:38.820 --> 09:41.520
But I know you have remained active politically.

09:41.720 --> 09:48.580
I mean, you, a few years ago, you helped get a bunch of Nobel Prize winners to sign a letter.

09:48.580 --> 09:59.240
I think more than 50, by signing a letter, what, to reduce military spending and instead use that money for dealing with climate change and poverty and, you know, real social and environmental issues.

10:00.140 --> 10:06.740
And I guess I'm wondering if that's, is that just an entirely separate enterprise from what you do as a physicist?

10:06.740 --> 10:09.500
Or are they connected in some way?

10:10.880 --> 10:14.140
Well, for me, they're connected because that's all me.

10:14.300 --> 10:34.880
And in a sense, when I, in my late 20s, got into full immersion into science and started spending my time doing calculations and being in front of Blackwood with my friends and colleagues for days, months, years, I was missing the social engagement.

10:34.880 --> 10:37.960
And I was even feeling a little bit guilty.

10:38.440 --> 10:39.760
It's not, I'm not going to change the world.

10:39.880 --> 10:40.560
I learned that.

10:41.180 --> 10:48.800
But yet, the good part of the world is thanks to a lot of people who have believed in a better world and worked for it.

10:49.080 --> 10:53.800
Worked for, I don't know, equality of human, abolition of slavery, abolition of death penalty.

10:54.500 --> 10:56.340
You know, democracy didn't come for free.

10:56.500 --> 10:58.380
People fought for it and so on.

10:58.380 --> 11:05.260
Now, more recently, I went back to some sort of engagement like the one you mentioned.

11:05.780 --> 11:07.220
Not very successful either.

11:08.040 --> 11:09.160
It's a second time.

11:09.600 --> 11:13.300
Well, in terms of if you actually, you know, get countries to reduce their military spending.

11:13.480 --> 11:13.600
Yes.

11:13.940 --> 11:19.820
Well, the idea was, you know, the idea of that thing and all this Nobel Prize, I got the signature of 60 Nobel Prizes.

11:19.820 --> 11:24.640
And the idea was not to ask your own government to reduce military spending.

11:25.100 --> 11:33.120
The idea was to ask your own government to start negotiating worldwide for a global common reduction of military spending.

11:33.980 --> 11:35.300
Keeping the balance of power.

11:35.780 --> 11:44.280
So asking, you know, my country decreased by 2% military spending, but also our enemies, so to say, decreased.

11:44.280 --> 11:49.900
So the most stupid things we humans do is to kill one another and fight.

11:50.320 --> 11:50.860
It's obvious.

11:51.020 --> 11:53.780
I mean, if we collaborate, everything gets better for everybody.

11:54.140 --> 11:57.380
And then we start fighting again because we're irrational.

11:57.580 --> 12:05.920
I think that humankind has just one single enemy, its own irrationality that pushes one against the other one.

12:05.920 --> 12:20.060
And I think the people like me who have the privilege of being paying for thinking, essentially, intellectuals in different kinds, have a duty of, you know, I've been paying for thinking.

12:20.180 --> 12:21.920
So let me tell you what I'm thinking.

12:22.460 --> 12:24.380
Not just on physics.

12:24.900 --> 12:25.760
Well, I mean, yes.

12:25.900 --> 12:29.780
I mean, I was struck by you said you were starting to feel a little guilty.

12:29.780 --> 12:36.340
You know, and I guess the question is, you know, feeling a little guilty if you weren't politically engaged in some way.

12:36.460 --> 12:44.820
And it raises the question of whether, no matter how cool, how amazing the science of quantum gravity might be, is...

12:44.820 --> 12:46.160
Is not the most important thing.

12:46.260 --> 12:47.260
Yeah, I mean, yeah.

12:47.380 --> 12:49.580
Is there an ethics to this?

12:49.760 --> 12:52.360
I mean, I don't want to get too heavy-handed here, but I don't know.

12:52.400 --> 12:53.280
How do you think about that?

12:53.340 --> 12:57.040
I mean, it's sort of the responsibility of the intellectual, the responsibility of the scientist.

12:57.040 --> 13:10.680
So the responsibility of the scientists, the physicists in particular, have the burden of the nuclear weapons that have the poison gift they're given to humankind, which are seriously risking today to destroy us.

13:11.860 --> 13:16.460
Look, my reading of the world is that we're going toward another catastrophe.

13:17.020 --> 13:23.240
The West has dominated the world for two or three centuries, is losing its power, but doesn't want to lose it.

13:23.240 --> 13:29.640
So it's military-dominating, it's imposing its will to the rest of the planet, the rest of the planet is not happy.

13:29.980 --> 13:32.820
This is a dramatically unstable situation.

13:34.060 --> 13:36.320
And everybody's demonizing everybody else.

13:36.500 --> 13:43.380
So for the Americans, the Russians are all very, very nasty, and so are the Chinese, and vice versa, and so on and so forth.

13:44.080 --> 13:47.080
We have in front of us a recipe for a total disaster.

13:47.080 --> 13:50.340
Now, humans sometimes are reasonable, right?

13:50.500 --> 14:02.900
I mean, the Soviet Union, the United States sat down and destroyed 80% of their nuclear arsenal, not little because of the push of scientists, the physicists, that, you know, feeling guilty for what they've done.

14:03.400 --> 14:06.300
They said, careful, I mean, just please be reasonable.

14:06.600 --> 14:13.820
And Gorbachev is on record of saying that the effect of the scientists pushed him toward that.

14:14.080 --> 14:15.660
So why are we not doing the same again?

14:15.660 --> 14:32.660
I think that in this moment in which belligerence is growing everywhere, any voice that raises and says, calm down, bring down the heat, discuss, sit, it's a good voice.

14:33.280 --> 14:33.840
Yeah, yeah.

14:34.080 --> 14:37.520
So we were talking earlier about the wonder of science.

14:37.620 --> 14:41.840
I mean, you were just saying it was just amazing, you know, when you first got into doing physics.

14:42.840 --> 14:44.640
Is there a politics of wonder?

14:45.660 --> 14:49.000
Ah, it is a politics of wonder.

14:50.360 --> 14:50.580
Huh.

14:52.500 --> 14:53.440
Probably, yes.

14:53.640 --> 14:54.060
I don't know.

14:54.120 --> 14:55.480
I never thought in these terms.

14:56.580 --> 14:57.740
You surprised me here.

14:58.460 --> 15:02.420
Wonder is something that makes you change, right?

15:02.760 --> 15:06.200
It's exactly what happens when you break out from your routine.

15:06.200 --> 15:12.520
You wonder because you discover a new idea, because you're a new person, because you're an experience.

15:13.520 --> 15:18.560
Wonder is, for Aristotle, is what starts the philosophical research.

15:19.660 --> 15:24.320
Wonder triggers curiosity and triggers desire to know.

15:24.320 --> 15:25.320
Right.

15:25.360 --> 15:28.280
I mean, he said all philosophy starts in wonder, I believe.

15:28.280 --> 15:30.140
He says all philosophy starts in wonder.

15:30.320 --> 15:30.400
Yeah.

15:30.400 --> 15:35.960
I mean, he says you wonder and then you start thinking and you start, but thinking mean not

15:35.960 --> 15:39.700
being content with the current worldview that you have?

15:39.700 --> 15:41.700
I think, yes.

15:41.760 --> 15:46.420
And in a sense, oversimplifying, there are two ways of going through life.

15:46.540 --> 15:48.820
One is that, well, I know the thing there is to know.

15:48.920 --> 15:49.920
I know what I have to do.

15:50.160 --> 15:52.340
And the world is what it is.

15:52.340 --> 15:56.540
And politically, what is around me, it is fine, no problem.

15:57.940 --> 16:02.500
And we are in a very good world from many perspectives, no doubt.

16:02.700 --> 16:08.920
We have a fantastically good world, even folding in the current world and, you know, 800 million

16:08.920 --> 16:12.000
people who don't have enough food today on the planet.

16:12.860 --> 16:20.140
But, you know, in past centuries it was worse, thanks to people who could not take the present

16:20.140 --> 16:27.940
for granted and dream something new because they had an idea that fascinated them and because

16:27.940 --> 16:29.460
they had wonder.

16:30.260 --> 16:32.740
And science is clearly motivated to wonder.

16:32.740 --> 16:34.280
You know, there's this funny thing.

16:34.440 --> 16:42.960
Scientists had to control their own emotions and they built up this picture of science, which

16:42.960 --> 16:44.360
is emotionless.

16:44.640 --> 16:44.740
Right.

16:44.820 --> 16:48.180
I mean, you're going to corrupt the science if you let your emotions into it.

16:48.180 --> 16:52.340
You're going to corrupt the science, which is to some extent is true, but emotions is

16:52.340 --> 16:55.820
the fool that pushes science.

16:55.960 --> 16:58.720
Otherwise, who would be interested in learning something else?

16:58.860 --> 17:02.760
Because you're curious, because you want to look at the planet and you say, why do they

17:02.760 --> 17:05.360
go around that way around us?

17:06.040 --> 17:08.180
You know, you study general activity, wow.

17:08.640 --> 17:11.740
And then you say, how does it go together with quantum?

17:12.160 --> 17:12.620
And so on.

17:12.620 --> 17:16.740
Is this, and it makes our life so much better, right?

17:17.020 --> 17:21.460
To some extent, children who wonder all the time have a wonderful life.

17:21.900 --> 17:22.120
Yeah.

17:22.320 --> 17:27.460
But I love your, when I, you know, asked about the politics of wonder, imagining what a better

17:27.460 --> 17:28.480
world would be.

17:28.620 --> 17:28.800
Yeah.

17:29.640 --> 17:33.060
I mean, so, because, you know, often wonder, we just think of it, oh, it's like you, you,

17:33.140 --> 17:36.500
you know, you walk out in a beautiful forest and that's wonderful.

17:36.500 --> 17:39.300
But it's, it's imagining the better world.

17:39.460 --> 17:40.080
That's, wow.

17:40.980 --> 17:41.200
Yeah.

17:41.540 --> 17:44.880
To imagine if there was no war and we could all live in peace.

17:45.220 --> 17:45.420
Yeah.

17:45.780 --> 17:47.540
So I have one final question.

17:47.540 --> 17:47.780
Yeah.

17:47.780 --> 17:49.420
Related to what we were just talking about.

17:49.540 --> 17:54.340
I mentioned that this podcast that we're starting called Wonder Cabinet, and we're just sort

17:54.340 --> 17:59.820
of playing around with this kind of imaginary exercise of, if there is an imaginary wonder

17:59.820 --> 18:02.620
cabinet, you know, a cabinet of wonders, cabinet of curiosities.

18:02.620 --> 18:09.580
In the modern age, in the 21st century, we're sort of trying to collect a library of wonders.

18:10.140 --> 18:17.360
So if there was like an object or a concept or an experience or whatever it might be, one

18:17.360 --> 18:21.280
thing that you'd want to put into that cabinet of wonders, anything come to mind?

18:22.300 --> 18:23.500
I don't know.

18:23.580 --> 18:28.940
I didn't think about that, but you pushed me to discuss, to ask so much about this idea

18:28.940 --> 18:30.320
that there's no ultimate reality.

18:30.320 --> 18:32.920
Can I put this notion into your cabinet of wonders?

18:33.100 --> 18:33.740
Yeah, absolutely.

18:34.680 --> 18:38.200
The absence of an ultimate reality, specifically.

18:38.640 --> 18:38.920
Okay.

18:39.320 --> 18:46.300
This idea that we live in a world of relations, which are sort of mirrors, reflecting mirrors.

18:47.980 --> 18:48.460
Wow.

18:48.580 --> 18:48.780
Okay.

18:48.800 --> 18:49.740
I have to think about that.

18:49.880 --> 18:55.160
That's the wonderful thing you would put in the cabinet of wonders, is forgetting, abolishing

18:55.160 --> 18:57.000
the idea that there is ultimate reality.

18:57.000 --> 19:05.160
Yeah, and I find it beautiful, liberating, sort of full of light, and powerful, incredibly

19:05.160 --> 19:05.660
powerful.

19:06.400 --> 19:10.260
It leaves space open for everything.

19:11.260 --> 19:13.000
It leaves freedom, in a sense.

19:13.520 --> 19:15.080
It's okay for you for the cabinet?

19:15.440 --> 19:16.500
You want something more concrete?

19:16.520 --> 19:17.900
No, no, that is wonderful.

19:18.020 --> 19:18.440
I love it.

19:18.500 --> 19:18.720
Okay.

19:19.900 --> 19:20.440
Thank you.

19:20.480 --> 19:21.360
This has been such a pleasure.

19:21.700 --> 19:22.080
Thank you.

19:22.140 --> 19:22.860
Thank you very much.

19:22.860 --> 19:27.960
That's the physicist, Carlo Rivelli, talking with us from the Island of Knowledge Think Tank

19:27.960 --> 19:32.900
near Siena, where scientists and philosophers and writers gather in an old chapel under the

19:32.900 --> 19:33.540
olive trees.

